## Summary

The terminal onboarding modal (WelcomeBanner) re-appears every time the user enters the terminal view because dismissal state is tracked per-backend-name in a React useState Set that reinitializes from localStorage on each mount. However, the real bug is that dismissal is keyed by backendName (e.g., "claude"), meaning a user must dismiss the modal once per backend type. The expected behavior is a single global dismissal: once the user dismisses or interacts with ANY welcome banner, the onboarding is considered complete and never shows again.

Additionally, because the dismissedWelcome Set is initialized from localStorage in a useState initializer, if TerminalView remounts (e.g., due to Suspense boundaries or navigation), the Set is rebuilt correctly from localStorage. So the per-backend dismissal via localStorage actually survives remounts. The real issue is the per-backend granularity: a user who dismisses "claude" still sees the banner for "codex" or "opencode" tabs, which feels like the modal "re-appears on each view entry" when switching between different backend tabs.

## Technical Approach

Change from per-backend localStorage keys to a single global key. Instead of N keys like terminal-welcome-dismissed-claude, terminal-welcome-dismissed-codex, use one key: terminal-onboarding-dismissed = "1". When set, suppress ALL WelcomeBanners regardless of backend.

This is the simplest fix with minimal code changes. The WelcomeBanner component itself needs no changes — only the state management in TerminalView.tsx changes.

### Key Design Decisions
- **Single boolean over per-backend Set**: The original design tracked dismissal per backend so each backend could show its own intro. However, the bug report says "show once globally" — after seeing any welcome banner, the user understands the feature and doesn't need it again.
- **Keep localStorage (not sessionStorage)**: Dismissal should persist across sessions permanently, matching the bug report's requirement.
- **Backward compatibility**: On first load after the fix, read both the old per-backend keys and the new global key. If any old key exists, treat as dismissed and write the new global key. This prevents the modal from reappearing for existing users.

## Files to Modify

### 1. internal/webui/frontend/src/components/TerminalView/TerminalView.tsx
**Changes:**
- Replace the dismissedWelcome useState<Set<string>> initializer that scans localStorage for terminal-welcome-dismissed-* keys with a simple useState<boolean> that checks for a single terminal-onboarding-dismissed key
- Add backward-compat migration: if any old terminal-welcome-dismissed-* key exists in localStorage, set the new global key and remove old keys
- Replace handleDismissWelcome(backendName: string) with handleDismissWelcome() (no parameter needed)
- Update the WelcomeBanner render condition from !dismissedWelcome.has(tab.backendName) to !dismissedWelcome (simple boolean check)
- Update the onDismiss callback to call handleDismissWelcome() without backendName
- Update the onExampleClick callback similarly
- Remove dismissedWelcome from the renderTerminalPane dependency array (or keep it as the new boolean)

**Specific state change:**
```typescript
// BEFORE: per-backend Set
const [dismissedWelcome, setDismissedWelcome] = useState<Set<string>>(() => {
  const set = new Set<string>();
  try {
    for (let i = 0; i < localStorage.length; i++) {
      const key = localStorage.key(i);
      if (key?.startsWith("terminal-welcome-dismissed-")) {
        set.add(key.slice("terminal-welcome-dismissed-".length));
      }
    }
  } catch { }
  return set;
});

// AFTER: single global boolean
const [dismissedWelcome, setDismissedWelcome] = useState<boolean>(() => {
  try {
    if (localStorage.getItem("terminal-onboarding-dismissed") === "1") return true;
    // Backward compat: migrate old per-backend keys
    for (let i = 0; i < localStorage.length; i++) {
      const key = localStorage.key(i);
      if (key?.startsWith("terminal-welcome-dismissed-")) {
        localStorage.setItem("terminal-onboarding-dismissed", "1");
        return true;
      }
    }
  } catch { }
  return false;
});
```

**Specific handler change:**
```typescript
// BEFORE
const handleDismissWelcome = useCallback((backendName: string) => {
  setDismissedWelcome((prev) => { ... });
  localStorage.setItem(`terminal-welcome-dismissed-${backendName}`, "1");
}, []);

// AFTER
const handleDismissWelcome = useCallback(() => {
  setDismissedWelcome(true);
  try {
    localStorage.setItem("terminal-onboarding-dismissed", "1");
  } catch { }
}, []);
```

**Specific render change:**
```typescript
// BEFORE
{tabHasConnected.get(tab.id) && !dismissedWelcome.has(tab.backendName) && (
  <WelcomeBanner
    ...
    onDismiss={() => handleDismissWelcome(tab.backendName)}
    onExampleClick={(text) => {
      instanceRefs.current.get(tab.id)?.pasteText(text);
      handleDismissWelcome(tab.backendName);
    }}
  />
)}

// AFTER
{tabHasConnected.get(tab.id) && !dismissedWelcome && (
  <WelcomeBanner
    ...
    onDismiss={handleDismissWelcome}
    onExampleClick={(text) => {
      instanceRefs.current.get(tab.id)?.pasteText(text);
      handleDismissWelcome();
    }}
  />
)}
```

### 2. internal/webui/frontend/src/components/TerminalView/__tests__/onboarding.test.tsx
**Changes:**
- Update tests that set per-backend localStorage keys to use the new global key
- Update test assertions checking per-backend dismissal to check global dismissal
- Add a migration test: set old per-backend keys, verify that the component reads them and treats as dismissed
- Add a test: dismiss on one backend, verify it doesn't show on another backend tab
- Remove any tests that assert per-backend independence of dismissal (they now conflict with the spec)

## Files to Create

None.

## Dependencies

- This bug fix depends on commit d1eb40d (Talk to Lead first-time onboarding) being merged to main first, since the WelcomeBanner component and related code don't exist on the current blaze/main branch yet.
- No external packages needed.

## Edge Cases & Error Handling

1. **localStorage unavailable** (private browsing): Already handled — catch blocks fall through to show banners every session (matches existing behavior)
2. **Backward compatibility**: Old per-backend keys are detected and migrated to the new global key on first load. Old keys are left in place (no need to clean up — they're harmless and removing them in a loop during initialization adds complexity)
3. **Multiple TerminalView instances**: The component is only mounted once in App.tsx (wrapped in display:none when not active, not unmounted), so there's no risk of competing state
4. **Split view**: Both panes share the same dismissedWelcome boolean, so dismissing in one pane hides the banner in the other pane immediately (desired behavior)

## Testing Strategy

### Unit Tests (onboarding.test.tsx)
1. **Fresh user**: No localStorage keys → banner shows on first connected tab
2. **Returning user (new key)**: terminal-onboarding-dismissed=1 in localStorage → no banner shown
3. **Migration**: Old terminal-welcome-dismissed-claude key exists → treated as dismissed, new global key written
4. **Dismiss persists globally**: Dismiss on "claude" tab → "codex" tab also suppressed
5. **Dismiss writes to localStorage**: After dismissal, terminal-onboarding-dismissed=1 exists in localStorage
6. **Auto-dismiss on keypress**: Still works (WelcomeBanner internal behavior, unchanged)
7. **Example click dismisses**: Click example → banner dismissed globally + text pasted

### Manual Verification
1. Clear localStorage, open terminal view → see welcome banner
2. Dismiss it, navigate away from terminal, come back → no banner
3. Open a new tab with a different backend → no banner
4. Refresh the page → no banner (persisted in localStorage)

## Summary

This task refactors CommentsSection from a stateless display component into a stateful container that serves as the primary comment system for IssueDetailPanel. It replaces inline comment rendering with CommentItem (hfsf0.6), adds two-level threading via parent_id, uses Map<number, Comment> for O(1 state management, integrates ReactionPicker (hfsf0.7) and CommentForm with replyTo (hfsf0.9), and wires SSE comment mutations to trigger full comment refetches. It also modifies IssueDetailPanel to delegate comment responsibilities to CommentsSection and simplifies ActivityLog to use CommentItem for its comment rendering branch.

## Technical Approach

### Architecture

CommentsSection becomes a \"smart\" container component that owns comment state, threading logic, reply state, and reaction picker state. It replaces the current pattern where IssueDetailPanel owns localComments state and ActivityLog renders comments inline.

**Current flow:**
IssueDetailPanel owns \`localComments\` state → passes to ActivityLog (interleaved with events) → CommentForm as sibling

**New flow:**
IssueDetailPanel passes \`initialComments\` to CommentsSection → CommentsSection owns Map<number,Comment> state → renders threaded CommentItem list + CommentForm (inside CommentsSection) + ReactionPicker. ActivityLog uses CommentItem for its comment rendering branch (but comments are not duplicated — IssueDetailPanel passes comments only to CommentsSection, and an empty array to ActivityLog, effectively making ActivityLog events-only).

### Key design decisions

1. **CommentsSection owns comment state via Map<number, Comment>.** The parent (IssueDetailPanel) passes \`initialComments: Comment[] | undefined\` which seeds the Map on mount and re-syncs on issue change. CommentsSection handles optimistic updates internally (add, edit, delete). This moves state ownership from IssueDetailPanel into CommentsSection, which is the natural owner since it handles all comment interactions.

2. **Two-level threading uses parent_id with flattening.** Top-level comments have \`parent_id === null/undefined\`. Replies have a non-null \`parent_id\`. If a reply points to another reply (a reply-to-a-reply), it is promoted to be a reply of the root parent, enforcing max two levels. Threading is computed via \`useMemo\` from the commentsMap. Replies are rendered indented (CSS margin-left) below their parent, sorted chronologically.

3. **SSE comment mutations trigger full refetch via callback.** Per the REVIEW FIX constraint, MutationPayload has no comment_id field, so fine-grained state surgery is impossible. When a comment mutation is received for the open issue, CommentsSection calls \`getIssue(workspaceId, issueId)\` to refetch all comments. The SSE flow is: useMutationHandler receives \"comment\" mutation → calls new \`onCommentMutation(issueId)\` callback → useIssues exposes this callback → App.tsx checks if the affected issue is currently open → calls IssueDetailPanel's \`onRefreshComments\` prop → CommentsSection refetches.

4. **CommentsSection includes CommentForm as a child.** This keeps reply state (replyToCommentId) self-contained within CommentsSection. CommentForm receives \`replyTo\` and \`onDismissReply\` props from CommentsSection's internal state. When a comment is added via CommentForm, CommentsSection optimistically inserts it into commentsMap.

5. **ReactionPicker state managed by CommentsSection.** A single \`activePickerCommentId: number | null\` state tracks which comment's ReactionPicker is open. When CommentItem fires \`onAddReactionClick(commentId)\`, CommentsSection sets this state. When ReactionPicker.onSelect fires, CommentsSection calls the addReaction API and updates its reactionsMap optimistically. Only one picker is open at a time.

6. **Reactions managed via optimistic local state.** Since there is no standalone \"get reactions\" API endpoint and getIssue() does not currently return reactions bundled with comments, CommentsSection maintains \`reactionsMap: Map<number, CommentReaction[]>\` as local state. For the \"add via ReactionPicker\" path, CommentsSection calls addReaction API and inserts the returned CommentReaction into reactionsMap. For CommentItem's internal reaction toggle (onReactionsChanged), CommentsSection triggers a full comment refetch which will pick up reaction state IF the backend is extended to return reactions. Until then, reaction toggles via CommentItem's built-in toggle may show stale data after refetch — this is acceptable for the MVP and is documented as a known gap requiring either a batch get-reactions endpoint or reactions bundled in the getIssue response.

7. **ActivityLog adopts CommentItem for its comment branch.** ActivityLog's inline comment rendering (lines 154-180) is replaced with \`<CommentItem />\` in read-only mode (no currentUser, no onReply, no onAddReactionClick — so no action row, no reactions). This is a minimal, backward-compatible change. However, in the primary use case, IssueDetailPanel passes an empty comments array to ActivityLog, making it events-only. The CommentItem adoption in ActivityLog is for any future use case where ActivityLog is rendered with comments.

8. **IssueDetailPanel passes \`onRefreshComments\` to CommentsSection.** This callback is fired when SSE indicates comments changed. CommentsSection calls \`getIssue()\` internally when this is triggered (or when its own \`refetchComments()\` is called). The \`onRefreshComments\` prop is a version counter that increments on SSE comment mutation — CommentsSection watches it via useEffect and triggers refetch when it changes. This avoids passing callbacks that CommentsSection would need to call at the right time.

### Trade-offs

- **Separating comments from ActivityLog timeline**: Loses the interleaved events+comments UX. But threading and interactive comments are incompatible with a flat timeline. A future task could add a \"unified timeline\" view toggle if needed.
- **Full refetch on SSE comment mutation**: Inefficient (fetches entire IssueDetails including non-comment data). But it's the only option without a comment_id in MutationPayload. A dedicated /comments endpoint could be added later to make refetch lighter.
- **Optimistic reaction state**: May diverge from server state between refetches. Acceptable for MVP; a batch get-reactions endpoint would fix this.

## Conventions Established

- CommentsSection follows the \"smart container\" pattern: owns state, delegates rendering to CommentItem
- Threading uses two-level flattening (parent + replies; reply-to-reply promoted to root)
- Comment refetch is triggered by a \`commentRefreshKey: number\` prop that increments on SSE mutation — CommentsSection watches it via useEffect
- ReactionPicker positioning: rendered inside the CommentItem's container div, positioned absolute above the \"+\" button via CommentsSection's wrapper div
- CommentForm is rendered as the last child of CommentsSection (not as a sibling)

## Files to Create

None. All changes are modifications to existing files.

## Files to Modify

### 1. \`internal/webui/frontend/src/components/IssueDetailPanel/CommentsSection.tsx\`

**Complete rewrite.** Replace the stateless display component with a stateful container.

**Imports:**
\`\`\`typescript
import { useState, useCallback, useMemo, useEffect, useRef } from \"react\";
import { getIssue } from \"@/api\";
import { addReaction } from \"@/api\"; // for ReactionPicker handling
import { useWorkspaceContext } from \"@/hooks/useWorkspaceContext\";
import type { Comment, CommentReaction } from \"@/types\";
import { CommentItem } from \"./CommentItem\";
import { CommentForm } from \"./CommentForm\";
import { ReactionPicker } from \"./ReactionPicker\";
import styles from \"./CommentsSection.module.css\";
\`\`\`

**Props interface:**
\`\`\`typescript
export interface CommentsSectionProps {
  /** Issue ID for API calls */
  issueId: string;
  /** Initial comments from IssueDetails (seeds the Map state) */
  initialComments?: Comment[];
  /** Current user identifier for edit/delete permissions (e.g., \"web-ui\") */
  currentUser?: string;
  /** Incrementing counter — when this changes, CommentsSection refetches comments.
   *  IssueDetailPanel increments this on SSE comment mutations. */
  commentRefreshKey?: number;
  /** Additional CSS class name */
  className?: string;
}
\`\`\`

**Internal state:**
\`\`\`typescript
// Core comment state — Map for O(1) lookups, seeded from initialComments
const [commentsMap, setCommentsMap] = useState<Map<number, Comment>>(() =>
  new Map((initialComments ?? []).map(c => [c.id, c]))
);

// Reactions state — optimistic local tracking
const [reactionsMap, setReactionsMap] = useState<Map<number, CommentReaction[]>>(new Map());

// Reply state — which comment is being replied to
const [replyToCommentId, setReplyToCommentId] = useState<number | null>(null);

// ReactionPicker state — which comment's picker is open
const [activePickerCommentId, setActivePickerCommentId] = useState<number | null>(null);

// Refetching state
const [isRefetching, setIsRefetching] = useState(false);
\`\`\`

**Sync effect — re-seed Map when initialComments changes (issue switch):**
\`\`\`typescript
useEffect(() => {
  setCommentsMap(new Map((initialComments ?? []).map(c => [c.id, c])));
  setReactionsMap(new Map());
  setReplyToCommentId(null);
  setActivePickerCommentId(null);
}, [initialComments]);
\`\`\`

**Refetch effect — triggered by commentRefreshKey changes:**
\`\`\`typescript
const prevRefreshKeyRef = useRef(commentRefreshKey);
useEffect(() => {
  if (commentRefreshKey === undefined || commentRefreshKey === prevRefreshKeyRef.current) return;
  prevRefreshKeyRef.current = commentRefreshKey;
  refetchComments();
}, [commentRefreshKey]);
\`\`\`

**refetchComments function:**
\`\`\`typescript
const refetchComments = useCallback(async () => {
  setIsRefetching(true);
  try {
    const issueDetails = await getIssue(workspaceId, issueId);
    if (issueDetails.comments) {
      setCommentsMap(new Map(issueDetails.comments.map(c => [c.id, c])));
    }
  } catch {
    // Silently fail — stale comments are acceptable until next refetch
  } finally {
    setIsRefetching(false);
  }
}, [workspaceId, issueId]);
\`\`\`

**Threading computation (useMemo):**
\`\`\`typescript
interface ThreadedComment {
  comment: Comment;
  replies: Comment[];
}

const threadedComments = useMemo(() => {
  const topLevel: ThreadedComment[] = [];
  const repliesByParent = new Map<number, Comment[]>();

  for (const comment of commentsMap.values()) {
    if (!comment.parent_id) {
      topLevel.push({ comment, replies: [] });
    } else {
      // Two-level flattening: if parent is itself a reply, promote to root
      let rootParentId = comment.parent_id;
      const parent = commentsMap.get(rootParentId);
      if (parent && parent.parent_id) {
        rootParentId = parent.parent_id;
      }
      const existing = repliesByParent.get(rootParentId) ?? [];
      existing.push(comment);
      repliesByParent.set(rootParentId, existing);
    }
  }

  // Attach replies and sort
  const chronSort = (a: Comment, b: Comment) =>
    new Date(a.created_at).getTime() - new Date(b.created_at).getTime();

  for (const thread of topLevel) {
    thread.replies = (repliesByParent.get(thread.comment.id) ?? []).sort(chronSort);
  }

  topLevel.sort((a, b) => chronSort(a.comment, b.comment));
  return topLevel;
}, [commentsMap]);
\`\`\`

**Comment callbacks:**

handleCommentAdded — optimistic insert into Map:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleCommentAdded = useCallback((newComment: Comment) => {
  setCommentsMap(prev => {
    const next = new Map(prev);
    next.set(newComment.id, newComment);
    return next;
  });
  // Clear reply state after adding a reply
  setReplyToCommentId(null);
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleCommentUpdated — optimistic update (from CommentItem edit):
\`\`\`typescript
const handleCommentUpdated = useCallback((updated: Comment) => {
  setCommentsMap(prev => {
    const next = new Map(prev);
    next.set(updated.id, updated);
    return next;
  });
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleCommentDeleted — optimistic soft-delete:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleCommentDeleted = useCallback((commentId: number) => {
  setCommentsMap(prev => {
    const next = new Map(prev);
    const comment = next.get(commentId);
    if (comment) {
      next.set(commentId, { ...comment, deleted_at: new Date().toISOString() });
    }
    return next;
  });
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleReply — set reply state:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleReply = useCallback((commentId: number) => {
  setReplyToCommentId(commentId);
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleDismissReply:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleDismissReply = useCallback(() => {
  setReplyToCommentId(null);
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleAddReactionClick — open ReactionPicker:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleAddReactionClick = useCallback((commentId: number) => {
  setActivePickerCommentId(prev => prev === commentId ? null : commentId);
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleReactionSelect — API call + optimistic update:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleReactionSelect = useCallback(async (emoji: string) => {
  if (activePickerCommentId === null) return;
  const commentId = activePickerCommentId;
  setActivePickerCommentId(null); // close picker
  try {
    const reaction = await addReaction(workspaceId, issueId, commentId, emoji);
    setReactionsMap(prev => {
      const next = new Map(prev);
      const existing = next.get(commentId) ?? [];
      next.set(commentId, [...existing, reaction]);
      return next;
    });
  } catch {
    // Silently fail — reaction not critical
  }
}, [activePickerCommentId, workspaceId, issueId]);
\`\`\`

handleReactionPickerClose:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleReactionPickerClose = useCallback(() => {
  setActivePickerCommentId(null);
}, []);
\`\`\`

handleReactionsChanged — on CommentItem's internal reaction toggle, trigger refetch:
\`\`\`typescript
const handleReactionsChanged = useCallback((commentId: number) => {
  // CommentItem toggled a reaction via its internal API call.
  // Trigger a full refetch to sync state. This is the same approach
  // as SSE-triggered refetch since we can't do targeted updates.
  refetchComments();
}, [refetchComments]);
\`\`\`

**Compute replyTo data for CommentForm:**
\`\`\`typescript
const replyTo = useMemo(() => {
  if (replyToCommentId === null) return undefined;
  const comment = commentsMap.get(replyToCommentId);
  if (!comment) return undefined;
  return { commentId: replyToCommentId, author: comment.author || \"Unknown\" };
}, [replyToCommentId, commentsMap]);
\`\`\`

**Render:**
\`\`\`tsx
const totalCount = commentsMap.size;
const rootClassName = [styles.section, className].filter(Boolean).join(\" \");

return (
  <section className={rootClassName} data-testid=\"comments-section\">
    <h3 className={styles.sectionTitle}>
      Comments{totalCount > 0 ? \` (\${totalCount})\` : \"\"}
    </h3>

    {totalCount > 0 ? (
      <div className={styles.commentList} data-testid=\"comments-list\">
        {threadedComments.map(({ comment, replies }) => (
          <div key={comment.id} className={styles.thread} data-testid=\"comment-thread\">
            {/* Top-level comment */}
            <div className={styles.topLevelComment}>
              <CommentItem
                comment={comment}
                reactions={reactionsMap.get(comment.id)}
                currentUser={currentUser}
                issueId={issueId}
                onCommentUpdated={handleCommentUpdated}
                onCommentDeleted={handleCommentDeleted}
                onReply={handleReply}
                onReactionsChanged={handleReactionsChanged}
                onAddReactionClick={handleAddReactionClick}
              />
              {activePickerCommentId === comment.id && (
                <div className={styles.reactionPickerWrapper}>
                  <ReactionPicker
                    isOpen={true}
                    onSelect={handleReactionSelect}
                    onClose={handleReactionPickerClose}
                  />
                </div>
              )}
            </div>

            {/* Replies (indented) */}
            {replies.length > 0 && (
              <div className={styles.replies} data-testid=\"comment-replies\">
                {replies.map(reply => (
                  <div key={reply.id} className={styles.replyComment}>
                    <CommentItem
                      comment={reply}
                      reactions={reactionsMap.get(reply.id)}
                      currentUser={currentUser}
                      issueId={issueId}
                      onCommentUpdated={handleCommentUpdated}
                      onCommentDeleted={handleCommentDeleted}
                      onReply={handleReply}
                      onReactionsChanged={handleReactionsChanged}
                      onAddReactionClick={handleAddReactionClick}
                    />
                    {activePickerCommentId === reply.id && (
                      <div className={styles.reactionPickerWrapper}>
                        <ReactionPicker
                          isOpen={true}
                          onSelect={handleReactionSelect}
                          onClose={handleReactionPickerClose}
                        />
                      </div>
                    )}
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    ) : (
      <p className={styles.emptyState} data-testid=\"comments-empty\">
        No comments yet.
      </p>
    )}

    {/* Comment form */}
    <CommentForm
      issueId={issueId}
      onCommentAdded={handleCommentAdded}
      replyTo={replyTo}
      onDismissReply={handleDismissReply}
    />
  </section>
);
\`\`\`

### 2. \`internal/webui/frontend/src/components/IssueDetailPanel/CommentsSection.module.css\`

