## Edge Cases & Error Handling

1. **Database unreachable at startup** — `runMigrations()` will throw, caught by `main().catch()`, logs fatal error and exits with code 1. This is correct — do not start serving if the DB is broken.

2. **Database unreachable after startup** — Health check returns 503 degraded. Better Auth requests that need DB will fail with their own error handling. The health check provides the signal for load balancers/orchestrators to stop routing traffic.

3. **Health check under load** — `SELECT 1` is a minimal query. No connection pooling concern since Drizzle manages connections. Health checks are excluded from request logging to avoid probe spam (matching Go pattern from `handlers_health.go`).

4. **CORS with no TRUSTED_ORIGINS** — Falls back to extracting origin from BETTER_AUTH_URL (same logic as task .3 auth.ts). This handles the common single-origin deployment case.

5. **CORS preflight caching** — `maxAge: 86400` (24 hours) matches the Go server preflight cache duration, reducing OPTIONS request volume.

6. **Large request bodies** — Hono has no default body size limit. Better Auth handles its own request parsing. For the auth service use case (OAuth callbacks, session cookies, token requests), request bodies are small. No explicit limit needed.

7. **Port already in use (main or metrics)** — `@hono/node-server` `serve()` will emit an error event. The `main().catch()` handler logs the error and exits. Clear error message needed for operators.

8. **METRICS_PORT same as PORT** — The Zod schema does not prevent this, but `serve()` will fail with EADDRINUSE on the second listener. The fatal startup error handler will catch this and log a clear message. A `.superRefine()` validation that rejects METRICS_PORT === PORT could be added but is not strictly necessary since the error is self-evident.

9. **Metrics counter overflow** — JavaScript `number` is a 64-bit float, accurate up to 2^53. At 1000 requests/second, overflow takes ~285 million years. Not a concern.

10. **Logger redaction edge cases** — The `redact()` function matches field names case-insensitively (e.g., `Authorization`, `AUTHORIZATION`, `authorization` are all redacted). Nested objects are redacted one level deep. Arrays are not traversed (log payloads should not contain arrays of objects with secret fields).

11. **Graceful shutdown during active OAuth flow** — If a user is mid-OAuth-redirect when SIGTERM arrives, `server.close()` stops accepting new connections but lets in-flight requests finish (Node.js default behavior). The OAuth callback may still complete if it arrives before the server fully closes. This is acceptable — worst case, the user retries the sign-in.

12. **Multiple SIGINT signals** — The shutdown handler uses a `shuttingDown` boolean flag to prevent double-close of the database connection.

13. **Better Auth rate limiter vs health/metrics** — Better Auth rate limiter only applies to routes handled by `auth.handler()` (i.e., `/api/auth/*`). Health is a Hono route outside Better Auth scope, so it is not rate-limited. Metrics is on a separate server entirely. This is intentional — health probes and metrics scraping should never be rate-limited.

14. **TLS warning spam prevention** — The `lastTlsWarning` timestamp ensures at most one warning per 60 seconds, even under high request volume. This prevents log flood from health probes or monitoring tools that do not set X-Forwarded-Proto.

15. **TLS warning false positives in dev** — The TLS middleware is only registered when `NODE_ENV=production`. Development and test environments will never see TLS warnings.

