## Edge Cases & Error Handling

### 1. OAuth error in URL query params
When Better Auth redirects back with `?error=access_denied` or similar, AuthGate extracts the error on mount via `new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('error')`. It cleans the URL with `window.history.replaceState({}, '', window.location.pathname)` to prevent the error persisting on refresh, and passes the error message to LoginPage for display.

### 2. returnTo validation (security-critical)
When restoring deep links from `sessionStorage.getItem('loom-auth-return-to')`:
- MUST start with `/` (relative path)
- MUST NOT contain `://` (prevents absolute URL injection / open redirect)
- If invalid, fall back to `/` — but since the fallback just means "don't redirect", actually do nothing (the app already loads at `/` after OAuth). Only call replaceState if the path is valid AND different from current path.
- Always remove the entry after reading (`sessionStorage.removeItem`)
- Use `window.history.replaceState({}, '', validPath)` for restoration (no navigation, just URL update)

### 3. Double-click on OAuth button
After the first click, set `loadingProvider` state that disables both buttons. The 10-second timeout re-enables them (sets `loadingProvider` to null, `timedOut` to true) with a "redirect seems stuck" message in case the redirect didn't fire.

### 4. Auth service down
If `useAuth().authServiceDown` is true, LoginPage shows "Authentication service is currently unavailable. Please try again later." and hides the OAuth buttons. No retry button needed — the auth context (.16) auto-retries session checks via useSession().

### 5. Race condition: isLoading flickers
On fast connections, `isLoading` may be true for only a frame. Returning `null` (not a spinner) means the user sees nothing for that frame — acceptable. The boot sequence (.21) already shows a blank screen during fetchAppConfig(), so this is a seamless continuation.

### 6. Browser back button after OAuth
After OAuth redirect + returnTo restoration, the browser history has: [original page] → [OAuth redirect] → [restored page]. The back button goes to the OAuth callback URL, which may re-trigger auth. This is standard OAuth behavior — Better Auth handles re-authentication gracefully.

### 7. sessionStorage not available
In rare cases (private browsing with storage disabled), sessionStorage.getItem/getItem may throw. The try/catch in AuthGate handles this — if storage fails, returnTo is simply lost (user lands on / after login). Acceptable degradation.

### 8. Multiple tabs
Each tab has its own AuthGate instance. If user signs in on tab A, tab B's useSession() (via Better Auth BroadcastChannel) updates automatically, causing AuthGate to re-evaluate and show children instead of LoginPage. No special handling needed.

### 9. signIn throws (network error before redirect)
If the signIn() promise rejects (e.g., auth service unreachable before OAuth redirect), the catch block in handleSignIn clears loadingProvider. The authServiceDown flag from useAuth() will update on the next useSession() check, showing the "service unavailable" message. The OAuth error won't appear in the URL (browser never redirected).

### 10. returnToHandled ref prevents duplicate restoration
The `returnToHandled` ref ensures returnTo is only processed once, even if React re-renders or isAuthenticated toggles. Without this guard, a fast re-render could read the (already-removed) sessionStorage key and attempt a redundant replaceState.

