**Add AbortController to the polling/fetch effect (~line 80):**
```typescript
useEffect(() => {
  mountedRef.current = true;
  const controller = new AbortController();

  // Wrap fetchData to pass the signal
  const fetchWithSignal = async (invalidateCache = false) => {
    try {
      const data = invalidateCache
        ? await refreshWorkspace()
        : await fetchWorkspace();
      if (mountedRef.current && !controller.signal.aborted) {
        setWorkspace(data);
        setError(null);
      }
    } catch (err) {
      if (!mountedRef.current || controller.signal.aborted) return;
      if (err instanceof DOMException && err.name === "AbortError") return;
      const message =
        err instanceof Error ? err.message : "Failed to load workspace data";
      setError(message);
    } finally {
      if (mountedRef.current && !controller.signal.aborted) {
        setIsLoading(false);
      }
    }
  };

  void fetchWithSignal();

  let intervalId: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;
  if (pollInterval > 0) {
    intervalId = setInterval(() => {
      void fetchWithSignal(true);
    }, pollInterval);
  }

  const handleVisibilityChange = () => {
    if (document.visibilityState === "visible") {
      void fetchWithSignal(true);
    }
  };
  document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", handleVisibilityChange);

  return () => {
    mountedRef.current = false;
    controller.abort();
    if (intervalId) {
      clearInterval(intervalId);
    }
    document.removeEventListener("visibilitychange", handleVisibilityChange);
  };
}, [pollInterval]);
```

Note: `fetchWorkspace()` and `refreshWorkspace()` in `workspace.ts` don't currently accept an `AbortSignal` — they use the module-level cache/dedup pattern. The `controller.signal.aborted` check in `fetchWithSignal` guards against stale responses being applied after abort. The underlying `get()` call inside `fetchWorkspace()` uses the default timeout signal but NOT the workspace abort signal — this is acceptable because the `cacheGeneration` counter in `workspace.ts` already discards stale in-flight responses, and the `mountedRef`/`controller.signal.aborted` guards prevent stale setState.

Full signal propagation through `workspace.ts`'s caching layer is unnecessary complexity for this task — the generation counter already handles the staleness concern at the cache level, and the abort check in the hook prevents state corruption.

