This task creates a Playwright E2E journey test that validates the full terminal-session lifecycle from an issue detail panel: opening an issue on Kanban, restoring a persisted terminal tab with split view, verifying the split detail summary and terminal pane, navigating to the Terminal view via NavRail, confirming tab persistence across views, and exercising Cmd+F search bar open/close. It replaces redundant per-component E2E tests for SessionNamePrompt, WelcomeBanner, SearchBar, and SplitDetailSummary by testing them as part of a realistic user journey.

## Technical Approach

**Architecture:** Single spec file using the existing Playwright fixture system (mockApi, mockSSE, appPage), with all API endpoints mocked via page.route(). No real backend needed.

**Key design decision — tab restoration vs. UI creation:** The current IssueDetailPanel add-tab dropdown only exposes a "Logs" option (line 1114-1146 of IssueDetailPanel.tsx). There is no "Terminal" option in the dropdown. Therefore, the test will validate the terminal tab via **mock persistence restoration** (mocking /api/issues/:id/tabs to return a saved terminal tab), which is the realistic production flow for returning users. This sidesteps the missing UI button and directly tests the critical path: tab restoration -> split view activation.

**Dependency on loomcli-ynv4y.4:** The split view guard at line 1156 requires activeTab.metadata.backend to be truthy. Currently, the restoration code (line 510-512) only reads session_name but not backend. Once ynv4y.4 lands (adding backend to IssueTab and the restoration map), the test will pass end-to-end. The plan includes the mock structure for the backend field so the test is ready once the fix merges.

**WebSocket mocking:** Terminal connection requires a token fetch (/api/terminal/token) then a WebSocket (/api/terminal/ws). Since we only need to verify the terminal pane renders (even in disconnected state per task description), we mock the token endpoint to return a dummy token and let the WebSocket fail naturally — the UI handles this gracefully with a "disconnected" state indicator.

**Screenshot strategy:** Use toHaveScreenshot() with descriptive names following the existing convention (e.g., split-view-layout.png, terminal-view-tab.png, search-bar-open.png). Viewport locked to 1280x720 per existing visual-regression convention.

